Today I spent some time exploring Token-2022 Program on Solana Explorer -  mainnet and looking at what actually happens on-chain instead of just interacting with the blockchain through code.
 
One thing I found interesting was exploring the Token-2022 program and comparing its activity across Solana's network.
 
What stood out to me was how much information is available publicly. From a single account or transaction, I can inspect things like instructions, accounts involved, signatures, fees, slots, and timestamps.
 
Coming from Web2, I'm used to databases where most of this information would live behind an API or internal dashboard. On Solana, I can open an explorer and inspect the underlying activity myself.
 
It also made the idea of a public blockchain feel much more real to me. The things I've been querying through RPC aren't abstract concepts—they're visible and verifiable on-chain.
 
Another small realization from this challenge:

An explorer isn't creating the blockchain data. It's making the data already on-chain easier for humans to understand.

The more I explore, the more the pieces from the previous days start connecting.